# Deploy to GitHub

Remote: `https://github.com/toreyakers5/Smart-Enrollment-.git`  
Branch: `main`

Deploy only when the user asks to deploy or push. Do not push on your own.

## Steps

1. Check `git status`, the diff, and recent commits. Do not commit secrets (`.env`, credentials, keys).
2. If there are uncommitted project changes, commit them as part of this deploy. Do not change git config. If Git has no identity, set `GIT_AUTHOR_NAME`, `GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL`, `GIT_COMMITTER_NAME`, and `GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL` for that command only. Use `toreyakers5` and `toreyakers5@users.noreply.github.com`.
3. `origin` must be the remote above. Add it if it is missing. Do not replace a different `origin` without saying so.
4. Fetch first. If GitHub is ahead, rebase local `main` onto `origin/main` and keep this app's README when the only remote file is the one-line stub. Do not force-push.
5. Push with a one-shot credential helper so the command does not change git config:

```powershell
git -c credential.helper=manager push -u origin main
```

6. If sign-in needs a browser or a two-factor code, stop and ask the user to finish that prompt.
7. Reply with the repository URL and the commit that was pushed.

```powershell
# Good: normal deploy
git fetch origin
git rebase origin/main
git -c credential.helper=manager push -u origin main

# Bad: rewriting GitHub history
git push --force origin main
```
